# {{FACTORY_NAME}}

A [jigs](https://github.com/salimhamed/jigs) factory: workflows and the service that runs them.

## Start

```sh
cp .env.example .env
pnpm install
pnpm exec jigs up --no-doctor
pnpm exec jigs run hello --input message=hello
```

For bare hello setup, `--no-doctor` skips the final doctor pass, which checks
GitHub credentials even when the workflow does not use GitHub. Postgres and the
service's machine prerequisites (including both agent CLIs) still apply. Once
integration credentials are configured, use plain `jigs up` and `jigs doctor`.


`hello` creates and removes a run directory, then returns its input. It needs no
repository binding, Linear token, GitHub token, or agent. The service still needs
its Postgres World and machine prerequisites reported by `jigs doctor`.
Configure integration credentials only when adding workflows that use them.

Every `jigs` below means `pnpm exec jigs`. The service listens on
`:{{SERVICE_PORT}}`, its dashboard on `:{{DASHBOARD_PORT}}`, and Postgres on
`:{{POSTGRES_PORT}}`. `jigs status` lists runs; `jigs status <run-id>` opens run details.
Run detail also lists resources recorded by the generated `registerResource` step,
independently of a workflow's return value.

## Layout and authoring

- `jigs.config.ts` registers deferred workflow imports, service settings, bindings,
  and schedules. Secrets live in `.env`.
- `jigs.ts` is generated durable step wrappers and bound blocks. Commit it;
  refresh it with `jigs generate` and keep custom code elsewhere.
- `workflows/hello.ts` is the trivial starting workflow. Add processes here with
  a `"use workflow"` function and a default export `{ workflow, inputs, requires }`.
- Add `blocks/` for reusable replay-safe coordination without directives.
- Add `steps/` for custom `"use step"` functions doing external work.
- `jigs.config.test.ts` checks declarations and compiled durable IDs.

Factory imports use `#jigs`, `#blocks/<path>`, and `#steps/<path>` from the
package's `imports` map. The config's workflow loaders stay relative.

## Add the ship recipe

```sh
jigs recipe list
jigs recipe add ship
```

The command copies the ship workflow, tests, and Linear block into this factory.
It reports `created` and `kept` files and preserves existing files. Registration
is manual: add the printed line under `workflows` in `jigs.config.ts`:

```ts
ship: () => import("./workflows/ship.ts"),
```

Copied source is yours to edit; package upgrades do not overwrite it. `ship`
reviews a Linear ticket, implements and reviews a change, opens a pull request,
handles review and CI, and requests release after merge. Failed runs can retain
files for `jigs resources list` and preview-first `jigs resources prune`. Set
`LINEAR_API_KEY`, configure the GitHub
identity, and install/authenticate its agent harnesses before running it.

```sh
jigs bind git@github.com:owner/repo.git
jigs up
jigs run ship --input ticket=ABC-123 --input binding=repo
```

See [delivery](https://github.com/salimhamed/jigs/blob/main/docs/delivery.md)
for model selection, budgets, and prompt overrides. Binding commands edit a
literal `bindings` object; computed configurations require manual edits.

## Verify and upgrade

```sh
pnpm typecheck
jigs build
pnpm test
```

Tests inspect the last build, so build first. `jigs up` applies changes to a
running service. `jigs upgrade` installs a release, refreshes `jigs.ts`, runs
`jigs up`, and typechecks custom code. Review and commit the generated changes.
GitHub Packages reads its token from `~/.npmrc`; this factory's `.npmrc` only
routes the scope.

Changing workflow or step paths or function names changes durable IDs. Finish
or cancel affected active and parked runs before deploying those changes.
